With an **adjacency matrix** representation, both take `O(V^2)` time, since checking all possible neighbors for each vertex requires scanning an entire row.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

Vertices are enqueued when first discovered and dequeued to process their neighbors, ensuring vertices are visited in **increasing order of distance** from the source — which is why BFS finds shortest paths in **unweighted graphs**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

With a simple array instead of a heap, it takes `O(V^2)` time, which can be faster for dense graphs.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Bellman-Ford''s algorithm** correctly handles negative edge weights (as long as there''s no negative-weight cycle) by relaxing all edges `V−1` times, in `O(V × E)` time.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

If any edge''s distance estimate can still be reduced in this extra pass, the graph contains a **negative-weight cycle** reachable from the source.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Prim''s algorithm** runs in `O(E log V)` time using a binary heap, growing the MST one vertex at a time by always adding the minimum-weight edge connecting the tree to a new vertex.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

Cycle detection is efficiently performed using the **Union-Find (Disjoint Set Union)** data structure.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It can only be performed on a **Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG)** — a graph containing a cycle has no valid topological order.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It is obtained by performing a DFS and pushing each vertex onto a stack when its DFS call **finishes** (all its descendants have been fully explored); popping the stack then gives the vertices in valid topological order.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

`Ω(g(n))` gives an **asymptotic lower bound** (best-case behavior), and `Θ(g(n))` indicates the function is **both** `O(g(n))` and `Ω(g(n))` — a tight bound describing the function''s exact growth rate.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

This matches **Case 2** of the Master Theorem, where `a=2, b=2`, so `n^(log_b a) = n^1 = n`, which matches the non-recursive work `f(n) = O(n)`, yielding `Θ(n log n)`.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

This matches **Case 1** of the Master Theorem: `n^(log_b a) = n^(log2 2) = n^1`, which **dominates** `f(n) = O(1)`, so `T(n) = Θ(n)` (this describes, for instance, a binary tree traversal with constant work per merge step).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

By the Master Theorem (Case 2, with `a=1, b=2`, `f(n)=O(1)=O(n^0)` matching `n^(log_b a)=n^0`), this solves to **`T(n) = O(log n)`**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Dynamic Programming** is used when subproblems **overlap**, so solutions to subproblems are **stored (memoized)** and reused, avoiding redundant recomputation — this is essential when the same subproblem would otherwise be solved exponentially many times.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

This is a **pseudo-polynomial** time algorithm, since its complexity depends on the numeric value `W`, not just the input size in bits.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

Using **memoization or bottom-up DP**, each of the `n` subproblems is computed once, giving `O(n)` time and `O(n)` (or `O(1)` with rolling variables) space.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

After sorting (`O(n log n)`), the greedy selection itself takes `O(n)`, giving overall `O(n log n)` time.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Dynamic programming** explores a space of choices more exhaustively (via subproblem solutions), guaranteeing a globally optimal solution even when greedy choices would fail.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Overlapping subproblems** means the recursive solution **re-solves the same subproblems repeatedly**; both properties together justify storing subproblem results (memoization/tabulation) for an efficient DP solution.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

This pruning makes it more efficient than brute-force search, which explores **all** possibilities regardless of whether partial solutions are already invalid.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**NP** is the class of problems whose solutions can be **verified** in polynomial time. **NP-hard** problems are at least as hard as every problem in NP (a solution to them could solve any NP problem), and **NP-complete** problems are those in NP that are also NP-hard — making them the ''hardest'' problems within NP.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

If `P = NP` were proven true, it would imply that every problem whose solution can be **verified** quickly could also be **solved** quickly, with enormous implications for cryptography and optimization.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

Other common examples include the **Vertex Cover**, **Hamiltonian Cycle**, and **Subset Sum** problems.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

They are preferred over BSTs because their **high branching factor** keeps the tree height very small, minimizing the number of expensive **disk I/O operations** needed to locate a record.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

In a **B+ Tree**, all actual data records are stored **only in the leaf nodes**, with internal nodes containing only keys for navigation, and leaf nodes are typically linked together in a list for efficient **range queries**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

Common resolution techniques include **chaining** (storing colliding elements in a linked list at that bucket) and **open addressing** (probing for the next available slot, using linear probing, quadratic probing, or double hashing).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

As `α` increases, the probability of collisions rises, degrading performance from the ideal `O(1)` toward `O(n)` in the worst case; hash tables are typically resized (rehashed) once `α` exceeds a threshold (commonly around 0.7).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

Although an individual resize-and-copy operation costs `O(n)`, doubling the capacity ensures that such expensive resizes happen **exponentially less often**, so the total cost over `n` insertions is `O(n)`, giving `O(1)` amortized cost per insertion.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

This allows **traversal in both directions** and `O(1)` deletion of a given node (without needing a separate pointer to its predecessor), unlike a singly linked list, which requires `O(n)` traversal to find the predecessor for deletion.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

Both insertion and extraction of the top-priority element run in `O(log n)` time, since each operation requires at most `O(log n)` sift-up or sift-down steps to restore the heap property.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A **complete binary tree** is one where all levels are completely filled except possibly the last, which is filled **from left to right** — this property is what makes array-based heap representation possible.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

This arithmetic indexing avoids the need for explicit pointers, saving memory.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

This property is frequently used to validate whether a given binary tree satisfies the BST property.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Inorder** visits left subtree → root → right subtree. **Postorder** visits left subtree → right subtree → root. All three traversals run in `O(n)` time using recursion or an explicit stack.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It is especially efficient for **prefix-based search operations** (like autocomplete), performing search/insert in `O(L)` time, where `L` is the length of the string, independent of the number of stored strings.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

An **adjacency list** requires `O(V + E)` space, making it far more space-efficient for **sparse graphs** (where `E` is much smaller than `V^2`).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It relies on tracking the **recursion stack (the set of nodes currently being visited in the current DFS path)**; if DFS encounters an edge to a node already present in this recursion stack, a **back edge** (and hence a cycle) is detected.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

An **unstable** algorithm may change this relative order (e.g., **Quicksort** and **Heapsort**, in their standard in-place forms).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It precomputes the **Longest Proper Prefix which is also a Suffix (LPS/failure function)** array for the pattern in `O(m)` time, which allows the algorithm to avoid re-examining characters of the text that have already been matched, unlike the naive `O(n×m)` approach.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

This can be improved to `O(1)` auxiliary space using an **iterative approach**, or in languages/compilers that support **tail-call optimization** with a tail-recursive formulation.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

After processing all elements, the root of this min-heap is the kth largest, achieving `O(n log k)` time, better than full sorting''s `O(n log n)` when `k` is small.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Ready** means the process is waiting for CPU allocation; **Running** means it is currently executing; **Waiting** means it is blocked on an I/O or event; **Terminated** means execution has finished.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A **thread** is a lightweight unit of execution **within a process**, sharing the same address space and resources with other threads of that process, making **context switching between threads faster** than between processes.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It is created when a process is created and used during context switches to save/restore process state.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**SJF** minimizes the average waiting time among all non-preemptive scheduling algorithms, but requires knowing (or estimating) burst times in advance, and can cause **starvation** of longer processes.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A **very small quantum** increases context-switch overhead (approaching pure time-sharing with high overhead); a **very large quantum** makes it behave like FCFS — choosing an appropriately sized quantum is essential for good performance.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Non-preemptive scheduling** allows a process to **run to completion or until it voluntarily yields** the CPU (e.g., **FCFS, Non-preemptive SJF**).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It is commonly resolved using **priority inheritance**, where the low-priority process temporarily inherits the higher priority while holding the contested resource.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It is prevented by enforcing **mutual exclusion** over the **critical section** using synchronization constructs like **mutexes or semaphores**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It requires knowing each process''s **maximum resource demand**, **currently allocated resources**, and the **total available resources** in advance.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Deadlock avoidance** (like the Banker''s Algorithm) allows all four conditions to potentially exist but makes **dynamic runtime decisions**, granting resource requests only if the resulting state remains safe.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A **counting semaphore** can take any non-negative integer value, used to control access to a resource pool with **multiple identical instances** (its value represents the number of available resource units).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It is typically solved using **two counting semaphores** (`empty` and `full` tracking available slots/items) plus a **binary semaphore (mutex)** for exclusive buffer access.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

One common solution is to impose an **asymmetric ordering** — e.g., having one philosopher pick up the right fork before the left (breaking the circular wait condition) — or allowing only `n-1` philosophers to sit simultaneously.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It allows programs **larger than physical memory to run**, enables efficient **multiprogramming**, and provides **memory protection/isolation** between processes.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

This solves the **external fragmentation** problem of variable-sized partitioning, though it can still suffer from **internal fragmentation** within the last page of a process.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It avoids the overhead of accessing the full page table in main memory for every memory reference, significantly speeding up address translation on a **TLB hit**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

The OS traps the fault, locates the required page on disk, selects a victim frame if memory is full (using a **page replacement algorithm**), loads the page into memory, updates the page table, and restarts the instruction.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**LRU (Least Recently Used)** replaces the page not used for the longest time and approximates optimal behavior well in practice. **Optimal** replaces the page that will **not be used for the longest time in the future** — it minimizes page faults but is only theoretically implementable (requires future knowledge) and used as a benchmark.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

This anomaly can occur with the **FIFO** page replacement algorithm but never occurs with **stack-based algorithms** like LRU or Optimal.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It typically occurs when the **degree of multiprogramming is too high**, so each process has too few frames to hold its working set, causing excessive page faults.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**External fragmentation** occurs when free memory is split into **many small, non-contiguous blocks** scattered throughout memory, such that no single block is large enough for a request even though total free space suffices (common in variable-sized/segmentation allocation).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

Segmentation can suffer from external fragmentation (like variable partitioning), while paging suffers from internal fragmentation.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It is used to determine how many frames a process genuinely needs to avoid excessive page faults, helping the OS decide the **degree of multiprogramming** and prevent thrashing.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**SSTF (Shortest Seek Time First)** picks the request closest to the current head position, reducing seek time but risking **starvation** of far-away requests. **SCAN (elevator algorithm)** moves the head in one direction, servicing all requests along the way, then reverses — balancing efficiency and fairness better than SSTF.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**C-SCAN** moves the head in **only one direction**, servicing requests along the way; upon reaching the end, it jumps back to the beginning **without servicing requests on the return trip**, giving more **uniform wait times** across all cylinder positions.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A **monolithic kernel** runs most OS services (file system, device drivers, etc.) in a single privileged address space for performance, while a **microkernel** keeps the kernel minimal, running most services as separate user-space processes for better modularity and fault isolation, at some performance cost.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A **library function call** executes entirely in **user mode** and does not require kernel intervention unless it internally invokes a system call.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It introduces overhead because no useful computation occurs during the switch itself, and it can also cause **cache and TLB misses** as the new process begins execution.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A **primary key** is the candidate key chosen by the designer to uniquely identify rows. A **foreign key** is an attribute (or set) in one table that references the **primary key of another table**, enforcing referential integrity.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

Functional dependencies are the foundation for normalization and identifying candidate keys.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

This is the most basic level of normalization, a prerequisite for all higher normal forms.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A relation is in **2NF** if it is in 1NF and has **no partial dependencies** — every non-prime attribute must be fully functionally dependent on the entire primary key.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A relation is in **3NF** if it is in 2NF and has **no transitive dependencies** — every non-prime attribute must depend **directly** on a candidate key.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

BCNF is **stricter than 3NF**: 3NF allows an exception where `Y` is a prime attribute even if `X` is not a super key, whereas BCNF has no such exception — every relation in BCNF is in 3NF, but not vice versa.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Consistency**: a transaction takes the database from one valid state to another, preserving all integrity constraints. **Isolation**: concurrent transactions do not interfere with each other''s intermediate states. **Durability**: once committed, a transaction''s changes persist even after a system failure.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

An **exclusive lock (X-lock)** permits only **one transaction** to both read and write the data item, blocking all other transactions from accessing it (read or write) until the lock is released.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

2PL guarantees **conflict serializability** of the resulting schedule, ensuring correctness of concurrent transaction execution.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

This additional restriction avoids the **cascading rollback** problem, where the abort of one transaction forces other transactions (that read its uncommitted data) to also abort.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Timestamp ordering** protocols avoid deadlocks entirely by assigning each transaction a unique timestamp at the start and ensuring conflicting operations are always executed in **timestamp order**, aborting and restarting any transaction that would violate this order rather than making it wait.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A schedule is **view serializable** if it produces the same ''reads-from'' relationships and final writes as some serial schedule; **every conflict-serializable schedule is view serializable**, but the converse is not always true (view serializability is a weaker, more general condition).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

Its three core components are **entities** (real-world objects, e.g., Student), **attributes** (properties of entities, e.g., Name, ID), and **relationships** (associations between entities, e.g., Student ''enrolls in'' Course).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A **weak entity** has no primary key of its own and depends on a **strong (identifying/owner) entity** for its identification, using a **partial key (discriminator)** combined with the owner entity''s primary key to form its full identity.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

The four common types are **one-to-one (1:1)**, **one-to-many (1:N)**, **many-to-one (N:1)**, and **many-to-many (M:N)**, where the last typically requires a separate junction/bridge table when mapped to the relational model.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A **LEFT OUTER JOIN** returns all rows from the **left table**, along with matching rows from the right table; if no match exists, the result contains `NULL` values for the right table''s columns.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**TRUNCATE** removes **all rows** at once, resetting identity counters, with minimal logging; it''s a DDL command. **DROP** removes the **entire table structure** along with its data from the database permanently.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**WHERE** filters individual rows **before** grouping occurs, while **HAVING** filters **groups** after aggregation, and can therefore reference aggregate function results (e.g., `HAVING COUNT(*) > 5`), which `WHERE` cannot do.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It is most commonly implemented using a **B+ Tree**, which supports efficient `O(log n)` search, insertion, deletion, and especially range queries.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A **non-clustered index** maintains a **separate structure** with pointers to the actual data rows, so a table can have **multiple** non-clustered indexes, though lookups require an extra step to follow the pointer to the actual row.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A sufficient condition for a lossless decomposition is that the common attribute set `R1 ∩ R2` must be a **super key** of either `R1` or `R2`.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Fourth Normal Form (4NF)** addresses this by requiring that a relation have **no non-trivial multivalued dependencies** unless `X` is a super key.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

Its main tradeoff is that highly normalized schemas often require **more joins** to reconstruct complete information, which can reduce query performance — leading some systems to deliberately **denormalize** for read-heavy workloads.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

An **uncorrelated subquery** is independent of the outer query and can be evaluated **just once**, making it generally more efficient.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**UNION ALL** combines the result sets **without removing duplicates**, making it faster since it skips the deduplication step.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

Triggers can execute **BEFORE** or **AFTER** the triggering event, and are commonly used for enforcing complex integrity constraints, auditing, or maintaining derived/denormalized data.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It is enforced through **foreign key constraints**, which can also specify actions like `ON DELETE CASCADE` or `ON DELETE RESTRICT` to define behavior when the referenced row is deleted.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Optimistic concurrency control** assumes conflicts are rare, allowing transactions to proceed without locking, but **validates** at commit time whether any conflicting changes occurred, aborting the transaction if a conflict is detected.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It is useful because, during recovery after a crash, the system only needs to **redo/undo transactions after the last checkpoint**, rather than replaying the entire log from the beginning — significantly speeding up recovery.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

In **WOUND-WAIT**, an **older** transaction requesting a resource held by a younger one **preempts (wounds)** the younger transaction, forcing it to abort, while a younger transaction simply **waits** for an older one.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Logical data independence** is the ability to change the **conceptual schema** (e.g., adding a new entity or attribute) without requiring changes to existing application programs or external views.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A **semaphore** is a more general synchronization primitive using an integer counter, which can be **signaled (released) by any thread**, not just the one that waited on it, and can control access to multiple instances of a resource.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Priority scheduling** is particularly prone to starvation, since low-priority processes may never be scheduled if higher-priority processes keep arriving; this is often mitigated using **aging** (gradually increasing a waiting process''s priority over time).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

The **physical address space** is the set of actual addresses in main memory (RAM); the **Memory Management Unit (MMU)** translates logical addresses to physical addresses at runtime, enabling virtual memory and process isolation.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A common mnemonic is ''Please Do Not Throw Sausage Pizza Away''.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It combines the OSI model''s Session, Presentation, and Application layers into a single **Application layer**, and its Network Interface layer covers the OSI''s Physical and Data Link layers.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

This gives the theoretical maximum **bit rate** achievable over a noiseless channel of bandwidth `B`.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

Unlike Nyquist''s formula, Shannon''s accounts for the degrading effect of noise on channel capacity.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

The receiver performs the same division; a **non-zero remainder** indicates that an error occurred during transmission. CRC can reliably detect burst errors up to the length of the CRC code.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Error correction** schemes, such as **Hamming codes**, add enough redundancy to identify the **exact bit position** of a single-bit error, allowing it to be corrected without retransmission.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

For example, for `m = 4` data bits, `r = 3` parity bits satisfy `2^3 = 8 ≥ 4+3+1 = 8`, giving a **`(7,4)` Hamming code** capable of single-bit error correction.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

Its main drawback is **poor channel utilization** on links with high **bandwidth-delay product**, since the sender remains idle waiting for each ACK instead of continuously transmitting.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

A larger window size increases channel utilization (up to the point where it matches the bandwidth-delay product), after which further increases provide no additional benefit.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Selective Repeat** retransmits **only the specific erroneous/lost frame**, requiring the receiver to buffer out-of-order frames, making it more efficient but more complex to implement.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Slotted ALOHA** divides time into discrete slots and requires transmission to start only at slot boundaries, doubling the maximum throughput to about **36.8%** (`1/e`) by reducing the vulnerable period for collisions.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It was primarily used in traditional **wired Ethernet (IEEE 802.3)** networks using a shared bus or hub, where stations sense the channel before transmitting and detect/abort upon collision — it became largely obsolete with the shift to switched, full-duplex Ethernet.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

Wireless LANs (IEEE 802.11) instead use **CSMA/CA (Collision Avoidance)**, which uses techniques like RTS/CTS handshaking and random backoff to **avoid** collisions rather than detect them after the fact.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

The subnet is determined by performing a **bitwise AND** between the IP address and the subnet mask, which separates the network/subnet portion of the address from the host portion.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

Subtracting the **network address** and the **broadcast address**, this leaves `32 − 2 = 30` usable host addresses.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

This improves **address utilization efficiency** and enables **route aggregation (supernetting)**, reducing the size of routing tables on the internet backbone.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**Link state routing** (e.g., OSPF) has each router **flood information about its direct links** to the entire network, allowing every router to independently compute shortest paths using an algorithm like **Dijkstra''s**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It is mitigated using techniques like **split horizon** (not advertising a route back to the neighbor it was learned from) and **route poisoning** (advertising an unreachable route with infinite metric).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It also ensures the final `ACK` of the connection termination reaches the peer, retransmitting it if the peer re-sends its `FIN` due to the ACK being lost.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

This ensures both sides agree on initial sequence numbers before data transfer begins.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

**UDP** is **connectionless** and **unreliable** (best-effort), with no handshake, no retransmission, and minimal overhead — making it suitable for latency-sensitive applications like video streaming and DNS queries.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

The **acknowledgment number** indicates the **next byte expected** by the receiver, cumulatively acknowledging all bytes received correctly up to that point.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

During **slow start**, `cwnd` begins small (e.g., 1 MSS) and **doubles every round-trip time** (exponential growth) until it reaches a threshold (`ssthresh`) or a packet loss is detected.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

After a **timeout**, TCP assumes more severe congestion, resets `cwnd` to 1 MSS, and restarts in **slow start** from scratch, also halving `ssthresh`.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

An **''A'' record** maps a hostname to its IPv4 address (an ''AAAA'' record is used for IPv6).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

It operates by broadcasting an ARP request on the local network; the device owning that IP address responds directly with its MAC address, which is then cached in the requester''s **ARP table**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

